# Local development only. Copy to .dev.vars (gitignored) to use it:
#
#     cp .dev.vars.example .dev.vars
#
# WARNING: DEV_AUTH_BYPASS=1 switches OFF the Cloudflare Access JWT check, so
# /api and /media requests are served without authentication. Put it ONLY in
# .dev.vars. Never set it in wrangler.jsonc, as a deployed variable or secret,
# or in CI.
#
# The bypass applies only when ALL of these hold (worker/auth.ts,
# worker/devPeer.ts, devserver/peerStamp.ts):
#   - the value is exactly "1";
#   - ACCESS_TEAM_DOMAIN and ACCESS_AUD are both unset (once they are set, the
#     bypass is ignored and the real JWT check runs);
#   - the request came through `pnpm dev` (vite dev) from THIS machine. The
#     dev server makes a random key per process, gives it to the Worker as
#     DEV_PEER_KEY, and stamps every request with that key and the
#     connection's TCP peer address, over anything the client sent. The
#     Worker wants the key AND a loopback peer, so a dev server started with
#     --host still refuses every other device on the network, whatever Host
#     header it sends. `wrangler dev` reads this file too but has no key, and
#     `pnpm preview` serves a build whose .dev.vars copy has this variable
#     stripped out, so the bypass never applies in either;
#   - nothing relayed it: no X-Forwarded-For, Forwarded, X-Real-IP or
#     True-Client-IP header, and no CF-Connecting-IP other than loopback, so a
#     tunnel or reverse proxy on this machine cannot hand it to the internet;
#   - the request's host is loopback (localhost, 127.0.0.1, [::1] or
#     *.localhost), so it can never open a *.workers.dev hostname.
#
# Responses served under the bypass carry the header X-Dev-Auth-Bypass: 1.
DEV_AUTH_BYPASS=1

# Optional: exercise the real JWT path locally instead of bypassing it.
# Setting both turns the bypass above off.
# ACCESS_TEAM_DOMAIN=https://<team>.cloudflareaccess.com
# ACCESS_AUD=<application audience tag>
